# Notes/Sync — the keys stored in secrets.sops.json, one value each (`sopsgen
# apply` fills generated ones, `sopsedit` the rest). Values built from other
# secrets live in env.nix. Key set must match secrets.sops.json and
# tests/fixtures/stacks/notes-sync.sops.json.

# rmfakecloud
# NEVER ROTATE, NEVER LEAVE UNSET: it signs tablet device tokens and the web
# session. Rotating re-pairs every tablet by hand; UNSET is worse — config.go
# autogenerates one, warns, and the NEXT RESTART silently invalidates every
# token. openssl rand -hex 32
JWT_SECRET_KEY=changeme_hex32
# The FIRST POST to /ui/api/login creates an admin from whatever arrives —
# notes-sync-init races to be first and the loopback publish is what stops
# anything else winning. NO HYPHEN OR PLUS in the email: sanitizeEmail runs on
# create but not on login, so a stripped character yields an unloggable account
# and a bare 401. openssl rand -base64 24
RMFAKECLOUD_ADMIN_EMAIL=idan@idanreed.com
RMFAKECLOUD_ADMIN_PASSWORD=changeme_strong_password

# Syncthing
# STGUIAPIKEY is a FULL-ADMIN credential that bypasses GUI auth; never let the
# Caddy vhost forward a client-supplied X-API-Key. Rotating it revokes nothing
# on its own — the key syncthing wrote into config.xml stays valid until a
# PATCH /rest/config/gui replaces it. openssl rand -hex 32
STGUIAPIKEY=changeme_hex32
# Seeded by the syncthing_generate one-shot. HALF A CREDENTIAL IS NO
# CREDENTIAL: leaving either blank turns GUI auth off rather than failing.
# openssl rand -base64 24
ST_GUI_USER=idan
ST_GUI_PASSWORD=changeme_strong_password

# Not secrets, but adjacent: key.pem in /mnt/fast/syncthing/config IS the
# device identity (losing it changes the device ID); peers are added by device
# ID from both sides, which is what makes publishing 22000 on 0.0.0.0 safe.
